  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Bright Sight: NTDs prevention and Eye Health Care for persons with disabilities and vulnerable groups</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Beneficiaries will have access to quality eye care and NTDs through decentralization of services across the country, strengthening specialist services and coverage in more remote areas. People with disabilities will have access to inclusive health services thanks to inclusive planning together with the most vulnerable categories such as women, children, refugees. In line with the IOVs, 90,290 people will have access to visits and treatments in 3 years.

Project beneficiaries are patients presenting with eye sight and eye related condition and surgeries of all ages and genders</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Bright Sight: NTDs prevention and Eye Health Care for persons with disabilities and vulnerable groups
To ensure health and well-being for all and for all ages in South Sudan, with particular attention to treatment and prevention in Eye Health and Neglected Tropical Diseases (NTDs), in line with targets 3.3.5 and 3.8.1 SDG 3.
Improve access to tropical neglected disease prevention (NTDs) and vision care services in Central Equatoria, Eastern Equatoria and Lakes, South Sudan, with a focus on people with disabilities and the most vulnerable, over 3 years.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The bright sight: NTDs Preventions and Eye Health Care for person with disabilities and Vulnerable Groups is a three years Funded project from the Italian Agency for Development Cooperation through Christian Blind Mission Italy, this project intends to reach approximately 150,000 beneficiaries through the provision of quality eye care services and NTD illness.  
The project was launched on 7th February 2023 in Buluk eye Centre Compound the Project Launch marked the official start of project activities to promote greater public eye care services in South Sudan.  During the launching Partners and Stakeholders shared their different experience and lesson learnt from the previous project implemented. The partners appreciate project&#x2019;s geographical scope and what the project will achieve and how it will benefit the communities. During the launch the project documents were shared with partners and Participants with the aim of providing some background to the project partners and the issues the project seeks to address.
The event brought about, 63(F=35, M=28 participants mainly from CBM partners Governments, and the community these include, CUAMM, CORDAID, ACROSS, OVCI, Buluk Eye Centre and Director of Health Central Equatorial, Eastern Equestrian and lakes state, State Minister of Health Lakes and representative of the National Ministry of Health</narrative>
  </description>
